WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.577 --> 00:00:02.866
- [Narrator] Nature has always been

00:00:02.866 --> 00:00:04.572
an integral part of my life.

00:00:04.572 --> 00:00:08.072
It’s the place where I feel the most at peace.

00:00:10.339 --> 00:00:12.223
People who have visual impairment

00:00:12.223 --> 00:00:15.454
really have very limited
access to the outdoors.

00:00:15.454 --> 00:00:19.292
To fix this problem, I
created a Braille trail.

00:00:19.292 --> 00:00:21.875
(upbeat music)

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:27.790
- [Woman] Meet Evan, a
19-year-old who founded

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:30.991
Nature for All, an
organization that helps provide

00:00:30.991 --> 00:00:34.182
nature experiences for
the visually impaired.

00:00:34.182 --> 00:00:35.653
There are numerous challenges that

00:00:35.653 --> 00:00:37.462
visually impaired people
face when they try

00:00:37.462 --> 00:00:40.202
to enjoy nature, like uneven footing

00:00:40.202 --> 00:00:42.836
and not being able to read the signs.

00:00:42.836 --> 00:00:44.067
- [Man] Feel the rope.

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:46.278
Now you should be able to guide yourself.

00:00:46.278 --> 00:00:48.577
- [Evan] A Braille trail has a guide rope

00:00:48.577 --> 00:00:52.137
and signage in Braille
so the vision impaired

00:00:52.137 --> 00:00:53.974
can walk along it unassisted.

00:00:53.974 --> 00:00:58.598
It highlights sensory experiences
such as touching leaves

00:00:58.598 --> 00:01:00.190
and textured bark.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:02.522
Now everybody can enjoy nature.

00:01:02.522 --> 00:01:04.029
- [Woman] He first came up with the idea

00:01:04.029 --> 00:01:05.049
to make his own Braille trail

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:07.540
after he saw one that was vandalized.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.067
- I was 11 years old when I started

00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:10.581
getting involved with Braille trails.

00:01:10.581 --> 00:01:13.780
I couldn't believe that
anybody would vandalize

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.272
a trail and take away the opportunity

00:01:16.272 --> 00:01:17.990
that the visually impaired so seldom have

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:20.862
to go out and experience nature.

00:01:20.862 --> 00:01:23.612
(people chatter)

00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:29.278
- My name is Marsha Farrow and
I have retinitis pigmentosa.

00:01:29.278 --> 00:01:30.706
- [Woman] Marsha has
been visually impaired

00:01:30.706 --> 00:01:31.766
since elementary school.

00:01:31.766 --> 00:01:34.991
- [Marsha] I have always loved nature.

00:01:34.991 --> 00:01:38.149
I feel that I can be
independent when I'm out

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:40.925
at a Braille trail and
that this is an area

00:01:40.925 --> 00:01:43.461
that I can enjoy and relax a little bit

00:01:43.461 --> 00:01:46.040
without having to be so anxious

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.207
about what's coming ahead.

00:01:49.484 --> 00:01:51.202
- [Woman] Since Evan
started his organization,

00:01:51.202 --> 00:01:52.592
Braille trails have been popping up

00:01:52.592 --> 00:01:54.676
all over the world and his website keeps

00:01:54.676 --> 00:01:56.432
tabs on their location.

00:01:56.432 --> 00:01:57.943
- Currently the website has over

00:01:57.943 --> 00:02:00.191
200 Braille nature trails
and sensory gardens

00:02:00.191 --> 00:02:03.838
in 35 countries on six continents.

00:02:03.838 --> 00:02:06.496
Accessible outdoor
opportunities can bridge

00:02:06.496 --> 00:02:08.993
the barrier between
people with disabilities

00:02:08.993 --> 00:02:11.154
and the world outside.

00:02:11.154 --> 00:02:12.909
- [Woman] And that goes for Evan, too.

00:02:12.909 --> 00:02:14.906
- [Evan] I've become a lot closer

00:02:14.906 --> 00:02:17.197
with the visually impaired community.

00:02:17.197 --> 00:02:19.119
They have such a positive outlook on life

00:02:19.119 --> 00:02:22.270
despite what they go through
and really they can do

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:24.438
just about anything that we can do.

00:02:24.438 --> 00:02:27.595
They are really my role models.

00:02:27.595 --> 00:02:30.178
(upbeat music)

